so I following very interesting speakers  today  and when I was asked to speak for a seat  bar he has an on-the-ground worker I  felt this was a very daunting task for  me I've taken much time and effort to  write a very well-written and curated  speech and when I realized this morning  that I couldn't remember a word of it I  made a very conscious choice and this  speech is getting put down because the  most important thing about today is  everybody in this room sharing their  experiences and what I'd like to do  today is share my story and share my  experience I don't have data to back me  up I don't have years and years of  wisdom and accumulated research that's  going to prove what I do is right but  what I do is I get to experience  everyday the light and children's eyes  as they give back to the world so I'll  start with my story and it actually  starts with me as a child and when I was  seven years old my father started  instilling giving in me and we've  already heard questions to Laura today  about you know when can you start  Laura started at the age of two with  children and and trying to understand  that giving in my family it was a  tradition my father was a doctor my  mother was a nurse and so they instilled  in us and every Christmas is my first  memory and my father would pack us all  up we put on your nice little Christmas  outfits I think mine was a reindeer  outfit and he'd take us down to the  hospital that he worked you know my  father's office never closed he worked  in a hospital it was open 24/7 and he  worked 24/7 but on Christmas Day he  didn't have to work he went into the  hospital because he chose to because his  patients were stuck in their rooms those  little rooms with dark settings they  didn't get to go home to the traditions  of family Christmases and so he brought  us along to give them a little injection  of Christmas  into the rooms and so we would stand  around at the edge of their beds and we  would start singing we wish you a Merry  Christmas and what I realized as I stood  there is you could actually see the  light in the patients faces you could  almost see the late in their hearts as  we sang away and we gave them this  little injection this little gift that  made their day a little bit better it  was my first act of volunteerism I had  the opportunity to give of my time on  Christmas Day with my family  and give a gift to somebody in need and  I realized something about that even at  the age of 7 it made me happy  giving actually made me happy even that  so move forward in time I get a little  older I can't continue doing my giving  with my family throughout my childhood  and I come into my 20s and I say okay  I've got the world at my feet it's an  open open oyster I can choose whatever I  want to do with my world and I had no  idea what I wanted to do I could become  a doctor like my father and nurse like  my mother but the one message that had  been driven back to me all of the time  was do something for a living that made  you happy well I kept going back to it I  realize the one thing that always made  me happy throughout my childhood was  giving so off I went and I started a  career on giving I became a special  event fund raiser and as you heard  earlier I had the opportunity to work  with some amazing people people just  like you in this room you're on the  ground workers you work in collaboration  with other people to do some good in our  world and so that's what I did and I  worked away as a special event fund  raiser for a number of decades and I  realized something in my journey that  every hour that I worked that I knew was  benefiting somebody else or a different  cause or a different organization it  continued to make me happy through the  years we've heard  a lot about the research that has been  going on with these two amazing people  today and the National Institute of  research talks about something called  the helpers hi I don't know if anybody  has actually heard about it here but we  have this little part in our brain our  prefrontal cortex of our brain and it's  actually been proven via MRI studies as  they watch the brain that even just by  thinking about altruism your brain is  activated it's activated in the same way  that you do through sex  recreational recreational drugs large  activity some of you may have heard of  the runner's high and in my case you  know a triple-layer chocolate fudge cake  which you know happens to be also food  but giving actually stimulates that same  part of your brain that gives you what  they refer to as the helpers hi so my  life moves forward and I'm given in a  remarkable gift one day I get a call and  I've become a mom pretty unusual not the  way most people you know become a mother  but my husband and I had decided that we  had wanted to adopt a child and that  call came one day and very shortly  afterwards I had the opportunity for the  very first time to hold this 11 week old  boy in my arms and that's when my world  truly changed forever that little boy  inspired me so much that I knew I had to  make the world a better place I don't  know how I was gonna do it I already  kind of thought I had done a lot through  my career but I realized I needed to  make the world a better place for him  and the other children of the world I  also knew as a parent that I wanted to  instill the same values in my child that  my parents had instilled in me I wanted  him to be kind I wanted him to be  compassionate I wanted to I wanted him  to experience what I had experienced and  was I wanted him to be happy and learn  it through acts of giving so at the ripe  old age of three I thought my son was  ready to learn to give I gave him a gift  on his third birthday and no it was not  a shiny new red bike I decided to start  a journey with him a journey of giving  every day for 365 days we committed to  doing one thing to give back to the  world  aka 365 gift I decided that to record  our journey I was gonna start a blog cuz  that's what everybody did at that time  just so you're very clear I'm not a  writer I'd never done social media I  wasn't on Facebook I had never done  Twitter but I decided that our journey  was so important to me that I wanted to  share it with as many people as I could  so every day I would get every day I  would blog and for 365 days that's what  we did we gave back and an amazing thing  happened surprisingly people read the  blog people actually engaged with  Twitter and Facebook they emailed us  from all over the world one of my  favorite stories and and comments that I  got back her email that I got was  actually from a teacher in Australia and  she emailed me one day she said you know  I've been following your blog I've been  following your journey she said and I've  actually now taken 365 give and I've put  it into my classroom this email  literally put me to tears I was amazed  that what my son and I were doing  together was actually inspiring a  teacher on the other side of the world  to teach her students to give as our  journey unfolded and actually came close  to an end I realized that our daily  giving had almost become an addiction  for me actually my husband would for  sure say it had become an addiction for  me but what I really  realized as we completed our journey was  that it gave my son and I a chance to  connect to our community it connected us  to other places around the world and  most of all our giving made us happy  together so an amazing thing happened on  day 365 as you heard earlier my husband  and I had been in the process of  adopting our second child throughout  this journey and on day 365 my son's  fourth birthday  my second son came home amazing well as  we said earlier imagine what you give  because the gifts may keep coming back  and bang  six months later my third son came home  well for a short time after that I was a  little bit busy in my world I was trying  to just get through the personal torture  of sleep deprivation having two little  boys under the age of one at the time  and one still not quite of the age of  five but I knew that I was still  committed to 360 365 give I knew that  one day I would find a way to take what  we did and transition it to another part  of the world I knew I still wanted to  give back to the world in some way  well year and a half into my motherhood  journey I was given the opportunity to  do just that I got a call from a friend  of mine her name Sarah Danaher and she  too was a mom and she was also a teacher  at a local school in west vancouver  pauline johnson french immersion school  and sarah said you know can we get  together i want to chat about something  and so we did what moms do we had a  playdate and we're down at a local beach  in West Vancouver our kids are happily  playing and Sarah says to me I want to  teach my kids what you taught your son  she knew and she had followed the blog  and she had followed our journey and she  had been an amazing supporter and she  felt that what my son and I had done  together was so important she wanted to  instill it in her stay  I was not only honored but humbled I was  excited and I was enthusiastic to see  where this might take us was it actually  even possible to take what we did and  put it into a classroom other than then  Sarah would there be any other teachers  that would even possibly consider this  so we sat down we did our research we  looked at the science of giving we  looked at the psychology of giving it's  actually how Laura and I'm met a number  of years ago we studied how we might be  able to take everyday giving and tie it  into the prescribed outcomes that  teachers have to attain in school with  their students and curricular outcomes  so we could tie daily giving directly  into their curriculum we took what we  thought was a pretty good pilot project  and we took it to her principal we sat  down with her a woman by the name of  Chantal Trudeau who happens to now be  the chair of my board of directors for  365 give and we told her about our pilot  program which we were going to name the  365 give challenge it happens to be a  really interesting tie-in with our  earlier talks on on competition and  challenging our students we were  actually doing something right which I'm  happy to hear we call that the 365 give  challenge and it went to our first goals  in our first 12-week pilot of the  program we had 300 students from the  ages of 5 starting at kindergarten up to  grade 7 they did it they went back their  teachers went back and they Kent started  to give and amazing things happened as  we watched their journey they got  engaged with their learning they were  happy to come to school because it was  their 365 give week at school their  teachers empowered them to choose what  they were gonna do to give back they  weren't told they had to donate to a  charity they weren't told  they had to volunteer the kids and the  teacher sat together and the kids got to  choose exactly what they wanted you  every day for five days so one week of  school one classroom together say the  least when the stories of the giving  started coming out of the school  we were amazingly surprised they help  support the SPCA  they volunteered their time at the local  Senior Center they picked up the garbage  on the beach and it continued one of my  favorite stories is in for day one the  100th day of school at the elementary  level they actually celebrate this day  in school especially at the elementary  level because it gives them an  opportunity to work with math with the  kids and so a grade two class who at  that point in that stage in your you  know your educational career you're just  learning to count to 100 so they decided  that they were gonna tie in the hottest  day of school to their daily gift they  wanted to make 100 dog biscuits to  donate to the local SPCA great idea so  as a parent I look at it both sides and  I'm like fantastic you know the kids are  learning to give but how is this  possibly tying into their education  aren't these kids supposed to be  learning math and science and how to  read and how to write  it's things we worried about all the  time so this is how it worked for the  kids they had to go into research and  find a recipe which they had to read so  they can learn how to make the dog  biscuits in comes they're reading and  writing and research skills even at the  age even in grade two then they had to  mix they did this right in school they  actually had to mix the batter to make  the cookies it tied right into their  science and they learned how baking and  food and science all connect together  they bake their biscuits well they had  to find out how many biscuits they  actually baked here's the neat thing  their goal was a hundred biscuits well  that day that grade two class they  exceeded their own expectations not only  did they  bake a hundred dog biscuits they  actually learned how to count to 212  that day because that's how many dog  biscuits they made which they donated to  the SPCA the stories haven't stopped  they keep coming last year we had 3000  students participate in the 365 gift  challenge 25 schools across the  Vancouver area it it now ranges from the  age of five to the ages of eighteen  student leadership programs are now  embracing it and they are teaching their  younger kids how to give as well one of  my other favorite stories is actually  about a kindergarten class so these are  five-year-olds their teacher their  teacher was teaching them about wildlife  and the ocean and she decided that she  would be able to intertwine their daily  giving and the kids decided that they  wanted to give back somehow to the  wildlife that live in the ocean so they  did a little bit more research and they  discovered that the aquarium had  recently taken in a sea otter by the  name of Wally Wally had been a very sick  sea otter and the aquarium took him in  because he could no longer survive out  in the wild and they brought him back to  health well these little five-year-olds  decided they were going to put on a  fundraiser for school they made posters  with pitchers they got on the school  announcements and started announcing  their fundraising initiative and they  were asked by their teacher to go home  and not ask their parents for money my  kids asked me for money all the time to  bring it a school they were actually  asked to go and do some work around the  house to earn the money so that the  donation was actually coming directly  from them with money that they had  earned well these little kindergarten  kids ended up raising $300 for the  aquarium I was so impressed I called the  aquarium myself and said I got to tell  you about these kids they're they're  amazing they've just raised three  hundred dollars for you and the aquarium  was thrilled they instantly sent bus  loads buses down to the school the next  week they picked up the children and  their teachers and they took them down  to the aquarium so that the kids  themselves could present their donation  right at the aquarium something for  everybody in this room to think about  because I know there is a number of  people here asking those questions for  themselves you know how do we engage new  people how do we get donations from  different types of people across  different borders  well the aquarium saw the opportunity  they saw the opportunity to engage a new  generation of givers that had the  potential of lasting an entire lifetime  so even though our children were  participating in the 365 gift challenge  and they did this particular donation  because of the 365 gift challenge a  really interesting thing happened that  summer and I actually got at via a blog  from the aquarium five of the kids from  that kindergarten class had birthdays at  summer and in lieu of receiving birthday  gifts when they had their birthday  parties they all asked for donations to  give to the aquarium to support Molly  even more between those birthday parties  those children not asked by their  teachers not asked by me  those children raised another additional  $400 for the aquarium so almost 800  dollars went to the aquarium by these  five years olds it was an amazing story  and the benefit that I get is that I get  to sit with these children I get to hear  their stories I get to see the smiles on  their face and they tell me the same  message over and over and over again  giving makes them happy I don't even  have to ask them they're not rewarded  for what they do they have found the  reward in the impact they make with  everybody that they touch and they have  now learned what I learned from my  father is that giving makes them happy  and I can only hope that they will carry  this lesson through their lifetime if  you can take away anything today I  challenge you to find the helpers hi I  challenge you today when you leave find  a way to give in your world  make it simple it can be simple pick up  the garbage that you walk past on the  street put it in the garbage help the  planet today the food bank was raising  money via a market out front go and  support before you go today find the  ways in your life that you can give  because I guarantee you you will get the  happiness back tenfold thank you so much  for having me today  [Applause]
 

